How to use this repo
  fork it
  in nvim.d/my/keymap.lua, comment out lines started with "cancel"
  prepare the environment according to the following 2 sections
  run ./install.sh
  read every files, modify and experiment

Key bindings

  tmux (prefix = Ctrl-b)
    Ctrl-o         = toggle opencode   (right pane, 60%)
    Ctrl-n         = toggle nvim       (left pane, 35%)
    Ctrl-/ Ctrl-_  = toggle db
    Ctrl-a         = toggle applogs
    Ctrl-g         = toggle focus
    Ctrl-s         = open-project      (browse repos)
    Ctrl-M-v / -f  = jump to nvim-config / farmweb
    Ctrl-M-s       = refresh project list
    Alt-H / L      = move between windows
    Alt-J / K      = move between panes
    Ctrl-b R       = reload tmux config

  vim
    Ctrl-j / k     = outdent / indent lines (normal + visual mode)
    Ctrl-j         = accept / confirm / open (similar to <cr>)
    Alt-j / k      = move in finder (nvim-cmp, telescope, fzf) and vim buffers
    Alt-H / L      = move in tmux windows
    Alt-K / J      = move in tmux panes
    Ctrl-h / l     = LSP prev issue / next issue
    gj / gk        = git next hunk / prev hunk

  shell
    Ctrl-c         = cancel the current input line

  Agent status dot (tmux status bar, before the window name)
    green dot  = that window's opencode finished its turn
    orange dot = that window's opencode is waiting for you (permission / feedback)
    the dot clears as soon as you select that window
    (set by the opencode tmux-bell plugin in the opencode-config repo)

Environment
  solve 'too many opened files'
    check
      ulimit -n
    fix (ubuntu 24.04)
      sudo vim /etc/sysctl.conf
        fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288
        fs.inotify.max_user_instances=524288
      sudo vim /etc/security/limits.conf
        * soft nofile 100001
        * hard nofile 100002
        root soft nofile 100001
        root hard nofile 100002
      reboot

  libssl.so is missing (ubuntu 22.04)
    libssl1.1 was dropped from the repos. Take any libssl1.1_1.1.1f-1ubuntu2.*_amd64.deb from
      http://nz2.archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/pool/main/o/openssl/
    sudo dpkg -i libssl1.1_*.deb && rm *.deb

  nodejs 20, eslint
    sudo apt remove nodejs libnode-dev libnode72
    curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -
    sudo apt install -y nodejs
    sudo npm install -g eslint

  neovim
    download latest nvim.appimage from https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases
    sudo mv nvim.appimage /usr/local/bin/nvim
    sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/nvim

  luarocks & pynvim
    sudo apt-get install python3
    sudo ln -s /usr/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/python    # required by luarock
    pip3 install --user --upgrade pynvim
    (in vim) <leader>ps    # to sync the packer cache

  build essential packages for compiling fzf-native
    ubuntu
      apt-get install build-essential
    mac
      xcode-select --install

  ripgrep: for live fuzzy searching
    sudo apt-get install ripgrep

  fdfind: for searching files by name (used in telescope as well)
    sudo apt-get install fd-find

  fzf: for project switching (tmux)
    sudo apt-get install fzf

  tmux
    download and extract latest tmux-xxx.tar.gz at https://github.com/tmux/tmux/releases
    sudo apt install libevent-dev libncurses-dev
    ./configure && make && sudo make install

  ghostty (terminal; GPU-accelerated via OpenGL on Linux, Metal on macOS)
    install (ubuntu 24.04)
      sudo add-apt-repository ppa:mkasberg/ghostty-ubuntu
      sudo apt update && sudo apt install ghostty
    ligatures supported; the font is configured in ghostty's own config

  fonts
    what the shipped configs expect
      ghostty.conf (active terminal)  = VictorMono Nerd Font Mono
      kitty.conf (unused)             = Fantasque Sans Mono Regular
    nerd fonts with icons (UI glyphs): https://www.nerdfonts.com/font-downloads
      download, unzip, move the ttf/otf into ~/.local/share/fonts
    fantasque (only kitty.conf needs it): https://github.com/belluzj/fantasque-sans/releases/latest
      download FantasqueSansMono-LargeLineHeight-NoLoopK.tar.gz, untar, move OTF/* into ~/.local/share/fonts
    paid option worth a look: https://www.myfonts.com/fonts/tabular-type-foundry/codelia/
    after any font install: fc-cache -fv

Optional for various LSP servers
  disable gnome animation
    gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface enable-animations false

  rust
    curl --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh

  lsd (rust based ls)
    https://github.com/Peltoche/lsd/releases      # grab the .deb for your arch

  ruby
    sudo apt-get install ruby

  ueberzug to preview images
    sudo apt install -y libx11-dev x11proto-xext-dev libxext-dev
    pip3 install ueberzug

  node packages (puppeteer → pug preview, svg-term-cli → recordings)
    npm install     # once, in this folder

  dependency for previewing font files (ttf, otf) — bin/fontpreview needs: xdotool sxiv convert fzf
    sudo apt-get install sxiv imagemagick xdotool

  pug linting
    sudo npm install -g pug-lint

  terminal recording
    sudo apt-get install asciinema
    usage
      asciinema rec --overwrite test.json
      asciinema play test.json
      cat test.json | npx svg-term --out test.svg
